## Why This Is Asked

Tests ability to design guardrails early in prompt pipelines, focusing on simple heuristics, mitigations, and verifiable safety.

## Key Concepts

- Risk scoring from prompts
- PII and system-prompt leakage detection
- Mitigations: redaction, output limits, guardrails
- Guarded prompts and verification

## Code Example

```javascript
function riskScore(prompt) {
  let score = 0;
  if (/\b(email|e-mail|phone)\b/i.test(prompt)) score += 3;
  if (/\b(export|download|CSV|exported)\b/i.test(prompt)) score += 2;
  if (prompt.toLowerCase().includes("system prompt")) score += 4;
  return Math.min(score, 10);
}
function mitigate(prompt) {
  return prompt
    .replace(/[A-Za-z0-9._%+-]+@[A-Za-z0-9.-]+\.[A-Za-z]{2,}/g, "[REDACTED_EMAIL]")
    .replace(/\b\d{3}[-.\s]?\d{3}[-.\s]?\d{4}\b/g, "[REDACTED_PHONE]");
}
```

## Follow-up Questions

- How would you adapt this for multi-tenant workloads with different policy baselines?
- What tests would you add to validate redactions without affecting legitimate outputs?

## Why This Is Asked

Tests ability to design scalable, policy-aware prompt orchestration with strong privacy guards and precise latency constraints, reflecting real-world multi-tenant systems at scale.

## Key Concepts

- Per-tenant policy binding
- Guardrails to prevent system prompt leakage
- Modular routing to multiple tools
- Determinism testing under latency budgets
- Structured audit for compliance and debugging

## Code Example

```javascript
// Minimal routing prototype (conceptual)
function routePrompt(prompt, tenant, policyStore){
  const policy = policyStore[tenant];
  const guarded = applyGuard(prompt, policy);
  return { plan: ["SQL-builder","DataMasker","SummaryFormatter"], guarded };
}
```

## Follow-up Questions

- How would you validate non-deterministic outputs across model variants under tight latency? 
- How would you scale tenant policies and audits across many tenants?

## Why This Is Asked
Tests ability to design practical routing logic for multi-tenant LLM services under latency and safety constraints.

## Key Concepts
- Per-tenant SLAs and data locality
- Model-version routing (fast/balanced/safety) and drift audits
- Lightweight policy engine and simple telemetry

## Code Example
```javascript
// Pseudo-routing sketch
function route(tenant, prompt){
  const policy = tenant.policy;
  const v = selectModel(policy, prompt);
  return {model: v, prompt: guard(prompt, v)};
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you test the routing policy under bursty traffic?
- How would you expand to learn from live drift feedback?
